All points that make an angle of $\frac{\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis lie on the
graph. [asy] unitsize(1 cm); draw(3*dir(240)--3*dir(60),red); draw((-2,0)--(2,0));
draw((0,-3)--(0,3)); label("$\frac{\pi}{3}$", (0.5,0.4)); label("$\theta =
\frac{\pi}{3}$", (2,1.8), red); [/asy] But all points that make an angle of $\frac{\pi}{3}
+ \pi$ with the positive $x$-axis also lie on the graph, since the radius $r$ can be
negative. Thus, the graph is a line. The answer is $\boxed{\text{(A)}}.$

Since $9 = 3^2$, we can convert directly to base 3 by expanding each base 9 digit into two
base 3 digits: \begin{align*} 8\_9 \&= 22\_3 \\ 1\_9 \&= 01\_3 \\ 3\_9 \&= 10\_3 \end{align*}
Putting the base 3 digit pairs together, we get $813_9 = \boxed{220110_3}$.
